1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
As I am sure everyone else feels the same way, no better place to be at 7.20pm through 10pm on a Monday night.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
And so we will proceed.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
So we finished up with signaling last week.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Actually you could spend many years studying signaling if you were so inclined.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
But this is all that we're going to do in this class on the subject.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
In my view there's still plenty more to do on it, so we shall see.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
But, and then we got a last topic.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
So the winner's curse.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Another fun and kind of surprising case where economists did actually add something to practical decision making.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Not surprisingly.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Moral hazard, like I said, everyone in church knew about moral hazard before economists started talking about it.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Adverse selection, probably a bit more influence from economics.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
But the winner's curse and other things, economists actually said something.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So, simple case.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
So suppose we start with a second price auction.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
A second price auction is one where the highest bidder wins and he pays the bid of the second highest bidder.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
So if the bids are 10, 9, and 5, who wins?

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
What is the money bid 10 and the winner pays how much?

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,000
9.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
That's a second price auction.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
And if this were a first price auction, who would win?

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
10.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And what would he pay?

24
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
10.

25
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,000
10.

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Now you may immediately realize there are different incentives for your bidding in a first versus second price auction.

27
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
So in a first price auction, if you truly value 10, do you want to bid 10?

28
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Probably not, because then even if you win you get no surplus.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
So you don't want to do that.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
On the other hand, as you're going to prove on the homework, if it's a second price auction,

31
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
then normally it makes sense to just bid your true value.

32
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Because you don't pay your true value.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
You pay the value of the second highest person.

34
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And you'll actually get a chance to prove that is a very good strategy.

35
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Normally.

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Yes.

37
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
So like if I want to blow up the boat and the guy bids 9, I could bid like 1,000.

38
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Yeah, exactly.

39
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
I don't think he's going to bid 1,000 once.

40
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Yeah, you could bid 10K and you still pay 9.

41
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Because I can drop out, right?

42
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
If he bids 1,000, I can drop out.

43
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
So hold on, this is a first or second price auction?

44
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
This is a second price auction.

45
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Yeah, second price auction.

46
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
But yeah, I mean you get the idea.

47
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
I could just bid 1,000 and then like assuming the guy is not going to want to bid 1,000 once,

48
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
he's going to have to pay at least 1,000.

49
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Yeah, exactly.

50
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So again, normally we picture this as sealed bids where you just write your number on a piece of paper

51
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,000
and then we sort them out.

52
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
But you could just as easily imagine that it's an auction where we raise the price by a penny a time

53
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
until finally there's only one person left.

54
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
And notice that's the same thing, right, for all practical purposes.

55
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
So again, imagine that we just start the price at zero and then we raise it by a penny per return

56
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
and then we finally get to $5.01 and this guy drops out, right?

57
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And then we raise it up and we get to $9.01 and then this guy drops out

58
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
and then this person here pays $9.01.

59
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Once there's only one person left, then that person wins.

60
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So it's just the difference of a penny.

61
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So anyway, first price auction is the one that we usually immediately think of

62
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
where you pay whatever you bid.

63
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
But second price auction is also effectively in pretty wide use

64
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
because any time you just raise the amount in small increments until only one person is left,

65
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
that's virtually the same as a second price auction.

66
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Might make sense.

67
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Okay.

68
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So imagine we got a second price auction and we got N participants.

69
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Now, of course there's many other fun auction rules.

70
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
There is the all-pay auction where everyone has to pay whatever they bid.

71
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Some highest bidder wins, but everyone pays whatever they bid.

72
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
So all the people who don't win pay their money and then get nothing.

73
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
You can do that.

74
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Why would anyone participate in an all-pay auction?

75
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Bids are likely to be lower.

76
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Yeah, bids tend to be really low in all-pay auctions,

77
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
so maybe you will get what you want for pennies on the door.

78
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Of course there's all the losers, but you always take a chance there.

79
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Okay.

80
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Now, we got N participants,

81
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
and then let's suppose that every bidder has rational expectations

82
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
about the true value of the item being auctioned.

83
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
So what does this mean?

84
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Well, it's something like an oil well, right,

85
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
where you're at least allowed to drill in an area and find the oil,

86
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
where the value to each person there is not based upon a personal interpretation

87
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
of the joy of the oil, right?

88
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
It's just based upon what is the market price of the oil that is below that ground.

89
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Similarly, this can even be true for art collecting.

90
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
If we suppose that you are all investors in art,

91
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
so you're not buying it for enjoyment.

92
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
You're buying it to sell on the market at some point.

93
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Okay, so that would work too.

94
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,000
All right.

95
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So suppose that everybody goes and has rational expectations

96
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
about the true value of the auction.

97
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So everyone is going to have their own valuation.

98
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
So your own valuation is VI, which is an equal to V,

99
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
which is the true value, plus your own personal error term,

100
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
your own random mistake, which could be high or low.

101
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Okay.

102
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
And then since we got rational expectations,

103
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
assume that the mean of the error is zero,

104
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
and it's got a variance of sigma squared.

105
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Okay.

106
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Now, since your estimate is unbiased by assumption,

107
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
it seems like the sensible thing is just to bid your true value.

108
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
As I said, on the homework you'll get a chance to prove this.

109
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
But intuitively, suppose that, you know,

110
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
let's go back to, you know, so we got like true values are 10, 9, and 5.

111
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
These are true values.

112
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Now, so this person here is, you know,

113
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
suppose this person here, they go and bid 10.

114
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
All right.

115
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
And then, of course, they're going to win,

116
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
because this person is not going to bid any more.

117
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
There's no reason to bid more than your true value, right?

118
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Right.

119
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
If you bid more than your true value, then there's two scenarios.

120
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
One is you lose, in which case what happens?

121
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Zero.

122
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Nothing.

123
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Right.

124
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Easy come, easy go.

125
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
On the other hand, if you bid more than true value and you win,

126
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
what has just occurred?

127
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
You lost.

128
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,000
You lost.

129
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Yes.

130
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
You have won the auction but lost the value race,

131
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
because you have paid, say, $9.50 for something you only vetted at $9.00.

132
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,000
That's stupid.

133
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And that's the second highest price below your true value.

134
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Yes.

135
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
I said, either you lose, in which case you're fine.

136
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Right.

137
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Or you win, in which case you regret it.

138
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Oh, I see.

139
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

140
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
You're absolutely right, which is why, yes, completely right,

141
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
which is why proving this is a little bit harder,

142
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
and that's why you get to do the homework.

143
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Anyway.

144
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
All right.

145
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So, anyway, so this person here,

146
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
so suppose that you're thinking about bidding $10.00,

147
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and then there's a question of what do you gain if you go and cut your bid?

148
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
All right.

149
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
So, again, if you bid $10.00, and, you know,

150
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
the other person has no reason to go above $9.00,

151
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
so if you bid $10.00, then you're going to wind up paying $9.00.

152
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Right.

153
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Now, suppose you cut your bid down to $9.80.

154
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Do you still win?

155
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
And what do you pay if you bid $9.80?

156
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
You still pay $9.00.

157
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
What if you go and you cut your bid to $9.50?

158
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
What do you pay?

159
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,000
$9.00.

160
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
What about if you cut your bid down to $9.01?

161
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
What do you pay?

162
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
$9.00.

163
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
What if you cut your bid down to $8.99?

164
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Then what happens?

165
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Then you lose, and you actually don't get the item.

166
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Right.

167
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
On the other hand, if you were to go and raise your bid, what do you get?

168
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Raising your bid doesn't get you anything either, right?

169
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
You could bid $1 million, and you still pay $9.00.

170
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Right.

171
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
So, anyway, that's the basic intuition for why it actually is least weakly dominant

172
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
for you to bid your true value.

173
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,000
All right.

174
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
So, anyway, suppose that everyone goes and bids their true value, right,

175
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and figuring, look, I've got rational expectations,

176
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
so if I bid my true value, what can go wrong?

177
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
What can go wrong?

178
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And it turns out that, surprisingly, given the setup of this problem,

179
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
if you win, you should expect to lose money.

180
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Why?

181
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Well, so if this is your estimate, so the estimate is going to be your view of the value,

182
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
your best guess of the value, equal to the truth plus the error,

183
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
and you know everybody else is the same, right?

184
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
So if you look at this, who's actually going to win the auction?

185
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
The person with the highest error.

186
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Yeah, the person with the biggest positive error is going to win the auction

187
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
if you've just bid your true value.

188
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
And then you lose a loss, right?

189
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
You basically lose, and you wind up paying that amount,

190
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
paying an amount equal to the true value plus the second biggest error.

191
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Now, here's the thing.

192
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
The average error is zero, but the average error of the winner is positive,

193
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
and that is the danger.

194
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
That is the danger because if you, you know, so, of course, on average,

195
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
you just bid it and you're fine, but you just bid your true value,

196
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
and if your error is zero, as it is on average, you just almost certainly lose, right?

197
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Because there's going to be someone with a positive error,

198
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
it could be a large group of people, right, a large group of bidders.

199
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
But if you win, on the other hand, you wind up overpaying.

200
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Okay, so that is the problem that is known as the winner's curse.

201
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Because if you win, you will wish you had lost.

202
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
If you win, you wish you had lost, but you know, be careful what you bid for,

203
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
you might get it.

204
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
So if you bid what seems like a good amount to you,

205
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
normally you just walk away with nothing,

206
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
but if you win it will be because you had a very high positive error,

207
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and therefore you wind up overpaying because, again,

208
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
you will pay the value of the second most deluded person,

209
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
most overly optimistic person.

210
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Does sellers having a negative error versus sellers just pricing low

211
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
to attract a number of buyers, or bidders rather,

212
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
does that come into this at all?

213
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I mean, you could just, like,

214
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
principally you could reverse it and make this,

215
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
so this is all for ease of buyers.

216
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
You could just easily reverse it and make it sellers,

217
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
where as long as the seller doesn't know the true value of what they're selling,

218
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
then the problem is that the ones that are going to offer the lowest price

219
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
are going to be the ones where they are most likely to have underestimated

220
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
the cost of doing it.

221
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
So it's totally symmetric.

222
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
So the more serious your error, the more likely you are to win.

223
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Where win does not mean make money.

224
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Win means be declared the winner of the auction,

225
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
which are two quite different things, right?

226
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Have you ever had the nightmare that you accidentally go to an auction

227
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
and you accidentally bid at the wrong time and then you owe $10 million?

228
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
How many Simpsons episodes are based on that?

229
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
I feel like there's been so many auctions on the Simpsons.

230
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
You just scratch your nose, but you accidentally put your bidding thing up,

231
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and sold to this idiot over here.

232
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
You're like, what? Sold.

233
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
This was like three weeks ago.

234
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
There was a bad auction episode.

235
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So that is the idea.

236
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
If you win, you're likely to make a serious error.

237
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Now, what can you do about this?

238
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Well, if everybody knew everybody's VI's, then there's a really easy solution.

239
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
So you just take the average of all of them,

240
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and then that gives you a better estimate of the truth.

241
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
So basically, if you could just go to every person and say,

242
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
what's your estimate of the true value? What's your estimate?

243
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Everyone will be honest.

244
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Then you just go and you take them all and average them all,

245
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and then on average that's going to be an accurate judgment.

246
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Of course, in the real world, you would not expect people to go around

247
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and answer truthfully whatever they believe the value of the object is, right?

248
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Okay?

249
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
Now, there is another thing that you can do.

250
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Another thing you can do, which is,

251
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
so of course you could actually release all the model,

252
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
or you could just notice, well, wait,

253
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
on average how much does the winner overbid by?

254
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Suppose that the winner overbids by an average of 20%

255
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
when they're doing it naively.

256
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Then all that you could need to do is take your estimate

257
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and say bid.8 of that.

258
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Well, let's see, hold on.

259
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
I said they're bidding 20% too high.

260
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
So that's 6 fifths.

261
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So actually you want to have 5 sixths.

262
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
So if you were just to go and bid 5 sixths,

263
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
then at least on average you would,

264
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
if you won, you would be glad you won.

265
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
We're going to be seeing how many of you bid 5 sixths of your estimate

266
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
that you're probably going to lose.

267
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Say, well, you'll lose, yes, you will.

268
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
So if everyone else keeps doing it, you'll lose,

269
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
but you'll be glad you lost.

270
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And then if it so happens that you win,

271
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
you will not be sad that you won.

272
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So it's better to lose auctions but not money

273
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
than it is to win auctions but lose money.

274
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
So anyway, basically, so intuitively,

275
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
if you look and see the winner is typically losing money,

276
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,000
then the obvious thing to do is to take your initial naive estimate

277
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and then deflate it so that you can once again expect to make money.

278
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
So, basic point.

279
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And then if you do win, you will not be burned.

280
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Of course, if everybody does this, then all the bids get depressed,

281
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
but then on average, the winner is going to be happy that he won.

282
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
If you deflate it enough.

283
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Now, how does this work in the real world?

284
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Well, so some economists went and looked at what was going on,

285
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and specifically they did bidding on petroleum,

286
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
or like potential petroleum, so an area believed in petroleum,

287
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and then they noticed there were patterns of persistent overbidding.

288
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
But then, before too long, what firms started doing was

289
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
not going and explaining this whole problem,

290
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
and it's not clear that they understood at all what was going on.

291
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
All they would do is come up with a naive estimate,

292
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and then just subtract a third, and then did that.

293
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
And if you ask them, why do you do that?

294
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
And they say, well, we went and calculated our thing,

295
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and then we just do our standard industry thing,

296
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
and subtracting a third at the end.

297
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
But why do you do that?

298
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Well, when we don't subtract a third, we lose money.

299
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
So this is one where they atheoretically hit on the right answer

300
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
without having much of a clear ability to explain what's going on.

301
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
So, I mean, this is something where the economists did not show up

302
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
and give them a new business strategy,

303
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
but they were able to show up and explain this weird fudge factor

304
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
happening at the second to last step,

305
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
where you just go, well, let's just go and subtract this thing

306
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
that everyone knows you have to subtract, or else you lose money.

307
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
So that's how they worked it out in practice.

308
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
So, let's see.

309
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Yeah.

310
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
So, the unrelated example, I remember in the state of New Jersey,

311
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:59,000
if you were hiring a mover, they would go and calculate out the rate,

312
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and then they would go and subtract somewhere between 40 and 60 percent

313
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
from whatever their second to last number was.

314
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
And I remember when I was asking them what's going on there,

315
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,000
and they said, well, so this is an interstate move,

316
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and so we're allowed to discount.

317
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I'm like, okay, but why is it like 40 to 60 percent?

318
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
And they said, well, it depends upon demand.

319
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So if it's a slack time, then we'll take off 60 percent.

320
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
If it's a regular time, like 50, peak time would be like 45 percent.

321
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
I'm like, why is it so big?

322
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Anyway, so when I looked into this,

323
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
the state of New Jersey does actually fix the prices for moves.

324
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
But then as long as it's a move between states,

325
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
you're allowed to put a discount on.

326
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
But if it were intrastate, in principle,

327
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,000
they would have to actually legally charge whatever that high number was.

328
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Now, it seems like a pretty crummy price control for it from anyone's point of view

329
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
because they basically self-assess each item, like how many boxes,

330
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and it didn't seem like there was any system

331
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
to make sure they put down the right number of boxes,

332
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
and I think it depended upon the size of the boxes.

333
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
You could just go and change large to medium boxes

334
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
and then get the price down that way.

335
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
But anyway, this is another case where they do all the math,

336
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and then the second to last step, enormous discount,

337
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
and then final number, and this is the easiest way to practice.

338
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
My question?

339
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
So the winner's curse does apply to like a first price auction as well, right?

340
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Yeah, it's just easiest to see in the case of a second price auction.

341
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And notice, it does not apply if it's just a variation on how much you like the product.

342
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
So if instead of epsilon i being an error,

343
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
instead it is just an amount of utility that you get,

344
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
then it's no problem.

345
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
So again, if the person that wins the Picasso auction

346
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
is the person who loves Picasso the most,

347
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
that's just a normal efficient outcome.

348
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
But what's interesting about this is we are actually assuming

349
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
that there's a true value that you're doing,

350
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
say, for a firm that just wants to resell the oil,

351
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and then the problem is not variation in the value you assign,

352
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,000
but a variation in your estimate of how much oil is there.

353
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
All right.

354
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And then if you read Lansbury's chapter on this,

355
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
which I think is assigned, I don't remember, but anyway,

356
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:13,000
he uncharacteristically misstates the theory,

357
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
possibly just for entertainment value,

358
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
but begins with, if you're reading this book,

359
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
you probably overestimated how good it is.

360
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And the idea is that when you're looking around for books on the shelf,

361
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
like what are the ones, you know,

362
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
who are the people who are going to pick up

363
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
the Armchair Economist off the shelf right away?

364
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,000
Are they going to be people who accurately forecast its value?

365
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Are they going to be the optimists?

366
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
That sounds great, and then you get it.

367
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
But anyway, that's only if you act naively,

368
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
but on the other hand, if you realize that whatever you're picking up

369
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
will tend to be worse than you expected,

370
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:50,000
then you will make an adjustment,

371
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
lower your expectations down to a reasonable level,

372
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
and then you would not actually be disappointed by the results.

373
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
All right, so you can make your own judgment

374
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
about whether you're good at this.

375
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Have you successfully lowered your expectations

376
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
down to a realistic level by this point in your life?

377
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
When you're a kid, like probably most people do,

378
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
most kids do have an overly optimistic view

379
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
of how much they're going to enjoy almost everything,

380
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and then things can get pretty disappointing for those kids.

381
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
I thought this was going to be the best birthday ever,

382
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
but it's only like the third best birthday.

383
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
All right.

384
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
All right, so questions about The Winner's Curse?

385
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Are you completely happy about The Winner's Curse and everything about it?

386
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Yes, happy about it.

387
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Intellectually satisfied by The Winner's Curse.

388
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:45,000
All right, in that case, we'll move on to efficiency implications.

389
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
So as I said, there are plenty of e-contact books,

390
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and I think they're still being written,

391
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
that say that when there's imperfect information, markets fail.

392
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
And this is a gross overstatement and demonstrably wrong.

393
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
It's like weird GMU lore that says that it's wrong.

394
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
But there are ironclad counter-examples

395
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
that every economist who can do math will say,

396
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
yes, that is a counter-example of the claim that markets fail

397
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,000
when there's imperfect information, so that claim is wrong.

398
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:15,000
All right, so general rules.

399
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
First, if the imperfect information is symmetric,

400
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
so we've got symmetric imperfect information

401
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
where nobody knows any better than anybody else,

402
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
then we've got market efficiency.

403
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:31,000
At least in the sense that there's nothing that anyone can do to improve anything.

404
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:36,000
All right, now, what if all market agents are equally informed,

405
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
but the government knows more than all the people in the market?

406
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Is that symmetric or asymmetric?

407
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
It's asymmetric, because to us, there's got to be anybody in the universe.

408
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
So if the government knows better than all the people in the market,

409
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
that would be a case of asymmetric information.

410
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
And you might very well say, well, of course, whenever a government says

411
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
there's a problem with imperfect information,

412
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:23,000
that's because the government knows better.


